Now I have enough context to generate the archetype selection. The diff is large (~2809 lines) covering:

1. `scripts/ship-pr.sh` — New deferred-push + behind-count + force-push logic in `_stage_and_push_ci_fixes`, rotation in `run_ci_fix_vendor`, new params on `run_rebase_rebump` family
2. `scripts/ci-behind-count.sh` — New helper extracted from `ci-status.sh`
3. `scripts/ci-status.sh` — Now delegates to `ci-behind-count.sh --no-fetch`
4. `scripts/hook-anti-read-poll.sh` — Extended from Read-only to Read|Bash with task-output polling detection
5. `AGENTS.md` / `skills/shared/orchestrator-never.md` — Policy docs
6. Test harnesses (`test-ci-behind-count.sh`, extended `test-hook-anti-read-poll.sh`)
7. `Makefile`, `CHANGELOG.md`, `docs/workflow-lifecycle.md`

Key risks: correctness in the rebase/push path, CI_FIX_REBASE_PENDING state management, the bash parsing in the hook, and the `--no-fetch` delegation to `ci-behind-count.sh`.

{"archetypes":[{"name":"rebase-state-machine","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The CI_FIX_REBASE_PENDING global and deferred-push path in _stage_and_push_ci_fixes introduce new state-machine edges with non-obvious ordering constraints (set on failure, cleared on success, force-push decision) that are easy to get subtly wrong.","prompt_body":"Inspect the `CI_FIX_REBASE_PENDING` global variable in `scripts/ship-pr.sh`: trace every code path that sets it to `true`, every path that clears it to `false`, and every path that reads it to choose between `git-force-push.sh` and `git-push.sh`. Verify that the value is always correct at each push site — specifically: (1) is it possible for `CI_FIX_REBASE_PENDING=true` to persist across iterations and cause an unintended force-push when no rebase occurred in the current attempt? (2) when `_stage_and_push_ci_fixes` returns a non-zero code via `return 1`/`return 4`/`return 2`, is the flag always in the right state for a subsequent retry push? (3) does the initialisation at line ~1109 (`CI_FIX_REBASE_PENDING=false`) cover re-entrant calls from `run_evaluate_failure`'s retry loop, or can a prior iteration's flag bleed into a fresh call? Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"behind-count-delegation","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"ci-status.sh now delegates behind-count to ci-behind-count.sh with --no-fetch, relying on a previously-completed fetch; if fetch timing or error handling diverges between the old inline computation and the new delegation, BEHIND_COUNT could be wrong.","prompt_body":"Compare the old inline behind-count in `scripts/ci-status.sh` (the removed `git rev-list "HEAD..$BASE_TARGET" --count` line at ~line 621) with the new delegation to `ci-behind-count.sh --no-fetch`. Verify: (1) the fetch at line ~80 of `ci-status.sh` uses the same `$BASE_REMOTE`/`$BASE_REF` values that are forwarded to `ci-behind-count.sh`, so `--no-fetch` is safe; (2) `ci-status.sh`'s awk parsing of `BEHIND_COUNT=` from `_behind_out` correctly handles the `lib-quiet.sh` output format (the helper emits on stdout or on FD 3?); (3) failure of the `ci-behind-count.sh` subprocess is handled (the `|| echo ""` branch sets BEHIND_COUNT to the awk no-match result, which then hits the `case ''|*[!0-9]*)` fallback — confirm this is correct); (4) in `ship-pr.sh` the call to `ci-behind-count.sh` uses the full fetch (no `--no-fetch`), not `--no-fetch`, which is intentional — confirm both call sites are consistent with the contract. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"bash-hook-parser","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The new Bash-tool hook logic in hook-anti-read-poll.sh uses POSIX string splitting (;;/&&/||) to parse arbitrary shell command bodies, which is a complex and fragile operation that can mis-classify commands or miss true task-output reads.","prompt_body":"Audit the Bash command parsing added in `scripts/hook-anti-read-poll.sh`: specifically `bash_line_task_output_poll_token`, `bash_normalize_cmd`, `bash_has_read_verb`, and `extract_bash_task_output_poll_token`. Check: (1) the `;;` / `&&` / `||` segment splitter in `bash_line_task_output_poll_token` — does it handle `||` correctly given the `case *'||'*)` pattern terminates with `;;` and `case *'&&'*)` shares the same variable `rest`; are there ordering ambiguities when a command contains both `&&` and `||`? (2) the sed backslash-newline join in `bash_normalize_cmd` uses `:a … ta` — does this port correctly to Bash 3.2 / macOS `sed`? (3) `bash_has_read_verb` uses grep-with-word-boundary patterns to detect `cat|tail|head|less|more` — are there false positives (e.g. `concatenate`, `headings`) or false negatives (e.g. `TaskOutput` tool)? (4) is there a risk that a legitimate non-polling Bash command containing `tasks/foo.output` in a comment or string literal could trigger the warning? Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"run-rebase-rebump-params","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"run_rebase_rebump and its two sub-helpers now accept defer_push and base_remote/base_ref positional arguments; the default-omitted call sites must remain byte-identical and the new defer-push path must skip git-force-push.sh without skipping the changelog commit.","prompt_body":"Examine every call site of `run_rebase_rebump`, `_run_rebase_rebump_from_step3`, and `_run_rebase_rebump_verify_plain_no_push` in `scripts/ship-pr.sh`. For each call site: (1) verify that existing callers that do NOT pass `defer-push` / `base_remote` / `base_ref` still produce exactly the original behaviour (parameter defaults resolve correctly in Bash `local phase=$1 defer_push=${2:-false} …`); (2) verify that the new `defer-push` path actually skips `git-force-push.sh` and does NOT skip the changelog commit or bump commit; (3) check whether passing the literal string `"defer-push"` as the second positional argument is the right convention (vs a flag like `--defer-push`), and whether any existing caller could accidentally forward a second positional arg that collides with this sentinel. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"waterfall-rotation","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"The tier-rotation change replaces a named cursor check with a waterfall_iter counter, which must preserve the first-fixer-non-health bail semantics exactly — keying off iteration 0 rather than the literal string 'cursor' changes which tier triggers the bail.","prompt_body":"In `scripts/ship-pr.sh` `run_ci_fix_vendor`, verify the refactored waterfall loop: (1) `waterfall_iter` is initialised to `0` before the loop and incremented *after* the first-fixer-non-health check — confirm the increment happens in exactly the right place so that the bail triggers only on the first tier of the rotated list regardless of which actual tier that is; (2) the `offset = start_attempt % 3` formula rotates `(cursor codex claude)` — confirm this is the intended rotation (attempt 0 → cursor, attempt 1 → codex, attempt 2 → claude, attempt 3 → cursor again); (3) `tiers` is a Bash array — verify this is Bash-3.2-compatible given `BASH_AUTHORING.md` forbids `mapfile`/associative arrays but does allow indexed arrays; confirm no `${tiers[@]:offset}` slice syntax that requires Bash 4; (4) verify `start_attempt` is propagated correctly from both `run_ci_fix_vendor` call sites in `run_evaluate_failure`. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
